reims-vgpu: the guest-rung blank census stops scanning a serve it already judged.

`note_guest_rung_blank` counts guest-memo serves that came back all zeroes
(lin_rung_guest_blank) with `rgba.iter().any(|&b| b != 0)` on every serve: a
byte-at-a-time scan that stops at the first non-zero byte, so content pays
little and a blank or mostly transparent span pays its whole length. Measured
(after reims-memo-arm-in-exec): with the memo's read and compare
down to ~45 ms in the worst drain second, `sampled_phase` resolve was still
743 ms there, ~670 ms of it outside every resolve_split part, at ~6.4 ms per
blank serve of a 1216x2688 RGBA16F layer (one full pass over 25 MiB); the
icon-press stalls of 0.3-1.5 s on the Home screen remained.

Now `rung_bytes_blank` answers the census. A serve the guest memo names by
its content generation (`LinearSampleIdentity::generation`, from the device
wide `next_sampled_content_generation`, so one generation is one set of
bytes) reuses the verdict its first serve computed
(`lin_rung_blank_verdict_reused`); at most 1024 verdicts are held and the
table is cleared when full. A serve without a generation, and the first serve
of each, scans 4 KiB blocks with an OR fold that stops only between blocks,
which compiles to wide loads. The census counts, their meaning and the host
cache comparison are unchanged; only how the blank verdict is reached.

Tests: draw::vulkan::vulkan_split_tests::the_blank_verdict_is_reused_per_content_generation;
the existing blank-census tests pass None and keep their counts.

